INSERT INTO career_courses (course_id, name, cluster, career_tag, demand, top_countries, board_exam, board_exam_name, duration_years, student_tip, ai_note)
VALUES ('OTH-010', 'AB/BS Foreign Service / International Relations', 'Other', 'DOMESTIC', 'Low-Moderate', ARRAY['Philippines', 'Singapore', 'Switzerland', 'Belgium', 'USA'], FALSE, NULL, 4, 'The DFA career is the most direct international deployment for this degree — study well for the FSOE (5 stages: written exam, essay, case study, committee exercise, psychological assessment). If the UN or multilateral track is your goal, a master''s in public administration, international relations, or economics is non-negotiable. Apply early to the ADB Young Professionals Program (closes in January each year, based in Manila) — it''s one of the best starting salaries and career tracks in Asia for development work. Learning French adds immense value for UN careers specifically.', 'Foreign Service/International Relations is primarily oriented toward Philippine government service (DFA diplomatic career) and post-graduate academic pursuits. While the degree teaches international affairs, the actual career track for international multilateral work is highly competitive and structurally requires: (1) master''s degree, (2) second language, and often (3) prior DFA or INGO experience. VIABLE INTERNATIONAL PATHWAYS: (A) DFA FSOE — the most direct. Pass the DFA Foreign Service Officer Examination (competitive 5-stage exam). As a Foreign Service Officer, you are deployed to Philippine Embassies, Consulates, and Permanent Missions abroad. This IS an internationally-deployed career — in Washington DC, Geneva, New York (UN), Tokyo, Beijing, Riyadh, Dubai, etc. — but it is a Philippine government career, not a foreign employer career. (B) UN/MULTILATERAL — UN Young Professionals Programme (YPP) is the most competitive international career track; requires master''s and passing a competitive written examination plus interview. ADB Young Professionals Program (YPP): highly selective (~30 spots/year globally); requires graduate degree + strong analytical skills; pays $70,000-90,000 starting; based in Manila but involves international travel and multi-country assignments. World Bank YPP: similarly selective; preference for economics/development focus. ASEAN Secretariat: hires professional staff (communications, policy, legal) from member states including Philippines; based in Jakarta; competitive. (C) BILATERAL EMBASSY LOCAL STAFF: Foreign embassies in Manila (US Embassy, Australian Embassy, British Embassy, Japanese Embassy) hire local Filipinos in political, communications, trade, and visa sections — these are ''locally employed staff'' positions, not diplomatic careers, but provide international exposure and competitive salaries. (D) INTERNATIONAL DEVELOPMENT ORGANIZATIONS: ADB (Manila-based), UNDP PH office, ILO PH office, World Bank PH office are all Manila-based international organizations employing hundreds of Filipinos in program analyst, research, communications, and project management roles.')
